c(87,[72],[103],'378–386','r=d(n−k+1)−2;2r≥dn−2',
'The stable-to-unstable comparison uses connectivity r=d(n−k+1)−2 and the condition2r≥dn−2, equivalent to n≥2(k−1)+2/d.',
'numerical computation in proof','E','Atiyah1961 Lemma6.4 p308 gives this connectivity calculation; the following theorem handles the remaining range by the divisibility restrictions on q_k.','1961','The algebraic inequality is correct; it cannot be silently strengthened by dropping the dimensional conditions.',['Atiyah1961'],comp='computations.md §18 redoes the inequality symbolically; calculations.py verifies the integer thresholds for d=1,2,4 and k=1,…,20.')
c(88,[72],[103,101],'387–394;240','THEOREM(6.5); n multiple of q_k',
'The Stiefel projection O_{n,k}→S^{dn−1} admits a cross-section iff q_k divides n; in the real case the printed theorem assumes n≥2k. The text expressly makes no assertion below that range.',
'proved by combination of preceding lemmas in reused typescript','E','Atiyah1961 Theorem6.5 p308 is identical. SigristSuter1973 p248 explicitly cites this theorem when identifying the quaternionic James number with the order of the real Hopf bundle in J(HP^{k−1}).','1961;later use1973','Established theorem with its express scope, not an unrestricted statement derived from a numerical experiment.',['Atiyah1961','SigristSuter1973'])
c(89,[71,73,87],[101],'242–245','determining ... q_k ... future publication',
'The reused author defers explicit determination of q_k and the relevant J-groups.',
'deferred programme in Atiyah typescript','B','AdamsWalker1965 intro and Theorem1.1 solve the complex cross-section criterion (the accessible primary extract states the exact necessity/sufficiency claim). SigristSuter1973 main theorem pp247–248 and Theorem2.6 p253 give the quaternionic criterion and explicit prime valuations, and explicitly connect it to Atiyah6.5. Their introduction identifies the earlier completed real problem. This does not settle CL-SA19-084.','Adams1962;Adams–Walker1965;Sigrist–Suter received16Nov1972,revised25Jan/20Feb1973,publishedOct1973','The numerical cross-section programme was developed into established results. The specific intrinsic-join comparison remains separately tracked.',['Atiyah1961','AdamsWalker1965','SigristSuter1973'])
c(90,[74],[108],'591–630','geometric connected components;½continue;[elle est continue]',
'The cancelled draft varies the number of geometric connected components in a universally open finite-type family, with properness and the strength of continuity uncertain.',
'wholly cancelled and revised statement','A','EGAIV3 Cor15.5.4 pp233–234 proves lower semicontinuity for proper universally open morphisms; Prop15.5.7 p235 proves local constancy near a geometrically reduced fibre. Remark15.5.8 gives failure without properness. The printed proof reproduces the connected-DVR reduction seen here.','1966','The nearest precise theorem is established in his published work; the cancelled reading cannot choose its hypotheses for us.',['EGAIV3'],'medium')
c(91,[75],[110],'722–742','ℓ(y) ... semi-continue supérieurement',
'For a proper morphism to a locally noetherian base, coherent F of full support, universally open at maximal points of the distinguished fibre and F_y satisfying S₁, upper semicontinuity of total geometric fibre multiplicity is proposed without assuming F flat.',
'open on the pages; proof partly sketched','C','EGAIV3 Thm12.2.1(xi), Lemma12.2.1.4 and Cor12.2.2(iii), pp179–181, prove the flat case. Remark12.2.3(vi), pp182–183, explicitly leaves replacement of flatness by full support and universal openness as a question. Searches for this exact EGA question and the associated-prime formulation found no later exact resolution; no exhaustive negative claim is made.','1966 publication of flat theorem and explicit remaining question','Flat version established. Ultimate status of the proposed universally-open extension not located in this audit. The counterexample to CL-SA19-093 does not refute this semicontinuity proposal.',['EGAIV3'],'medium')
c(92,[75],[110],'744–752;756–769','Lemme6.8; separate t-power torsion N from G=F/N',
'Over a DVR, the torsion submodule N of F yields a flat quotient G and an exact sequence0→N/tN→F/tF→G/tG→0; this reduces a proposed special/generic multiplicity inequality to the flat quotient provided its special fibre has the needed absence of embedded associated points.',
'proof sketched, conclusion expressly questioned','C','The short exact sequence follows from Tor₁(G,R/t)=0; EGAIV3 Lemma12.2.1.4 proves the flat inequality with S₁ special fibre. EGAIV3 Remark12.2.3(vi) explains why removing flatness still requires an associated-prime argument. The handwritten missing summation index is not repaired here.','1966','The torsion-separation step is valid. Its use does not prove the questioned general inequality without controlling associated points of G/tG.',['EGAIV3'],comp='computations.md §19 checks the Tor sequence and explains the precise unresolved transfer of hypotheses.')
c(93,[75,76],[110],'753–754;766–769','Si l’inégalité est vérifiée, alors F est plat',
'The literal sentence says that satisfaction of the special/generic length inequality implies flatness at maximal special-fibre points.',
'false as literally transcribed; later proof discusses equality','C','Counterexample: R=k[[t]],X=Y=SpecR,f=id,F=R⊕k. Both fibres have dimension0, the special fibre is S₁, support is X and its only component dominates Y; f is proper and universally open. Lengths2≥1 satisfy the inequality but t has kernel k on F. EGAIV3’s established flat inequality does not imply a converse from a mere inequality.','current independent verification19Nov2026;published comparison1966','Refuted literally. The possible word equality is a scan-reading question and is not adopted as a theorem.',['EGAIV3'],comp='computations.md §19 proves every hypothesis and Tor₁≅k; calculations.py records the finite length/torsion arithmetic.')
c(94,[75],[110],'780–784','Question liée ... m∈Ass(M/tM)',
'For a finite full-support module M over a noetherian local ring(A,m), with a one-dimensional minimal component and t avoiding specified primes, the author asks whether m is associated to M/tM; the ink may say associated rather than minimal primes.',
'open on the pages; hypotheses uncertain','C','EGAIV3 Remark12.2.3(vi) pp182–183 prints the closely matching question with t avoiding minimal primes, explicitly not assumed M-regular. If the manuscript instead assumes t avoids all associated primes, the assertion follows from depth(M)≤dim(A/p)=1 for a minimal p and the regular-element depth formula. The stronger no-regularity question was not resolved by the searched literature.','1966 printed question;regular-element case standard','The two readings have materially different difficulty. Keep the published question and the elementary regular-element case separate; present ultimate general fate as not located.',['EGAIV3','Stacks0338'],'medium',comp='computations.md §19 states the regular-element proof and its exact extra hypothesis.')
c(95,[77],[115],'944–979','R^pf_*F ≃ R^p(fg)_*O_{X′}(n)',
'For a proper morphism of locally noetherian schemes and a coherent sheaf, higher direct images are coherent. The page uses a projective Chow modification, Serre vanishing and a Leray collapse as part of the standard dévissage.',
'proof fragment on reused typescript','A','EGAIII1 Thm3.2.1 pp116–117 contains the same proof, including the projective birational g, large twist and vanishing of higher R^qg_*; Stacks02O3 gives a modern precise proof. This does not establish injective preservation for the handwritten Néron functor.','1961','Established in his published work, with properness, noetherian/coherence and large-twist hypotheses retained.',['EGAIII1','Stacks02O3'])
c(96,[78],[116],'1019–1023','equidimensionnalité de1.1ii);égalité dans2.6',
'A cancelled remark asks whether equidimensionality assumptions in missing numbered statements are essential.',
'cancelled open question; referenced statements absent','F','EGAIV3 §§12.1–12.2 gives exact openness and counterexamples showing that equidimensionality/S₁ hypotheses cannot indiscriminately be removed. The absent statements1.1(ii),2.6 were not identified uniquely by searches, so these examples do not answer the exact cancelled question.','1966 comparison','Not identifiable at theorem level until the preceding typescript is recovered.',['EGAIV3'],'low')
c(97,[78],[116],'1023','Prop2.8 Continuité des Cohen-Macaulay',
'The cancelled heading announces continuity/openness of the Cohen–Macaulay fibre condition, without an accompanying statement.',
'cancelled heading, not a complete theorem','A','EGAIV3 Thm12.2.1(vii), pp179–180, proves openness of the locus of Cohen–Macaulay fibres for proper finitely presented morphisms and finitely presented flat coherent modules; local version12.1.1(vi) precedes it. This is a precise published comparison, not a recovery of the missing wording.','1966','A standard nearby theorem is identified; missing hypotheses remain a documentary limitation.',['EGAIV3'],'medium')
c(98,[80],[121],'1237–1257','χ_A(L•)=χ_A(H•L);χ additive',
'For a bounded complex of finite-length modules, the alternating sum of term lengths equals that of cohomology lengths. Euler characteristics of coherent sheaves are additive when all required cohomology modules have finite length.',
'proof given on reused typescript','A','EGAIII1 OIII11.10.2 p49 proves the complex identity; III2.5.2 p109 proves coherent-sheaf additivity. The two short exact sequences for cycles/boundaries give the cancellation on the page.','1961','Established with finite-length and boundedness hypotheses.',['EGAIII1'],comp='computations.md §20 supplies the telescoping proof; calculations.py checks a concrete bounded complex by exact matrix ranks.')
c(99,[80],[121],'1258–1267','χ_A(X,F(n)) polynomial;leading coefficient≥0',
'Over an artinian local base, for a projective scheme and a very ample line bundle, χ(F(n)) is a rational-coefficient polynomial for all integers n and agrees with length H⁰(F(n)) for n≫0. Its leading coefficient is nonnegative; strict positivity requires a nonzero polynomial/sheaf condition.',
'stated on reused typescript; margin suggests strict inequality','A','EGAIII1 Thm2.5.3 pp109–110 gives the precise all-integer polynomial and eventual-H⁰ assertion. The zero sheaf has zero polynomial and prevents an unqualified strict-positivity assertion.','1961','Established; the marginal > cannot replace ≥ without an explicit nonzero hypothesis.',['EGAIII1'],comp='computations.md §20 gives the zero-sheaf test and standard projective-space Hilbert-polynomial examples; calculations.py prints those examples.')
c(100,[81],[129],'1431–1434','k[t₁,…,t_n]_m regular of dimension n',
'A polynomial algebra in n variables over a field, localized at a maximal ideal, is a regular local ring of dimension n.',
'proof sketched in wholly cancelled draft','E','Stacks0338 gives the flat local depth formula and the regular-fibre framework; EGAIV3’s local algebra presupposes these standard polynomial-ring facts. The index t₀ rather than t₁ would change the variable count to n+1 and is separately flagged.','classical commutative algebra;EGA1960s','Established with the literal variable count fixed; the draft’s residue-field reduction is compressed and is not silently extended to inseparable base-change arguments.',['Stacks0338','EGAIV3'],'high',comp='computations.md §23 gives the dimension/depth formula and variable-count check.')
c(101,[81],[129],'1436–1447','dim B_M=dim A+ν;ν=n−trdegκ(M)/κ(m)',
'For a noetherian local A and a prime P of A[t₁,…,t_n] lying above its maximal ideal, dim(A[t]_P)=dim A+dim(κ(m)[t]_{̅P}); the fibre term is n−trdegκ(P)/κ(m). For a maximal ideal over m the transcendence degree is0.',
'calculation in cancelled draft','E','The local flat dimension formula and the dimension formula for polynomial algebras give exactly this expression; Stacks0338 verifies the parallel depth formula. The page switches between maximal and general-prime language, so the apparent nonzero transcendence degree is a scope alarm.','classical/EGA-era dimension theory','Established reconstructed formula; the two statements coincide when M is genuinely maximal.',['Stacks0338','EGAIV3'],comp='computations.md §23.')
c(102,[81],[129],'1460–1465','prof B_M=prof A+ν;coprof B_M=coprof A',
'For the flat local polynomial localization with regular closed fibre of dimension ν, depth B_P=depth A+ν, hence dimension−depth is unchanged.',
'stated without proof in cancelled draft; final equality incomplete','A','Stacks0338 Lemma10.163.1 proves the flat local depth formula and explicitly attributes it to EGAIV2 Cor6.3.1. Together with CL-SA19-101 this gives the coprofondness identity. The exact EGAIV2 original locator was followed through the verified Stacks proof; its separate PDF was not read here.','EGAIV2 publication1965;modern official proof checked2026','Established under the noetherian, finite-module and flatness hypotheses.',['Stacks0338'],comp='computations.md §23 derives the subtraction.')
c(103,[81],[129],'1467–1470','Soit P l’une des propriétés......',
'The cancelled final corollary proposes ascent of an unspecified list of properties under polynomial extension/localization.',
'open/incomplete in cancelled draft','F','EGAIV3 and Stacks0338 support concrete regular/Cohen–Macaulay/depth consequences, but the blank property list prevents identifying the proposed universal claim. No source can fill this blank solely from neighboring words.','EGA1960s;modern proofs','Not a definite theorem; plausible examples are reconstructions, not restored text.',['EGAIV3','Stacks0338'],'low')
c(104,[82],[137],'1754–1774','F→B⊗_AF;A∩Ba=a;SpecB→SpecA',
'For a flat ring map A→B, faithful flatness is equivalent to surjectivity on spectra, nonzero fibres at maximal ideals, faithful tensoring and injectivity of every module’s unit map. Faithful flatness implies contraction IB∩A=I; conversely contraction of all proper ideals plus flatness gives faithful flatness.',
'proof fragment on reused typescript','E','Stacks00HP Lemma10.39.15 and the surrounding Stacks00H9 section prove the fibre/faithful-flatness criteria. SGA1 ExposéVIII develops faithfully-flat descent. The page’s B h(A) is a quotient-reading alarm.','standard flat descent1960s;SGA1 seminar1960–61/publication1971','Established; flatness is essential in the equivalence list.',['Stacks00HP','Stacks00H9','SGA1'],comp='computations.md §24 gives the unit-map and ideal-contraction proofs.')
c(105,[83],[126],'1378–1390','Faisceaux mous;FM_I,FM_II;constant sheaf example',
'Softness asks that sections over suitable closed subsets extend; a nontrivial constant sheaf on a connected space can fail it when a disconnected closed subset carries different prescribed values.',
'definition and example in reused typescript','E','Grothendieck1957 §3.3 p159 discusses soft/flasque sheaves and their acyclicity; Cartan1951XV develops the related fine-sheaf machinery. The manuscript uses local versions of extension axioms, so a global softness claim requires the stated separation/paracompactness hypotheses.','Cartan1951;Grothendieck1957','The extension obstruction is elementary; the exact typed textbook provenance remains unidentified.',['Grothendieck1957','Cartan1951XV'],comp='computations.md §21 tests X=[0,1],closed subset{0,1},values0 and1.')
c(106,[83],[124],'1333–1338','THEOREME3 partition de l’unité',
'A soft sheaf of rings on a paracompact Hausdorff space admits a partition of unity subordinate to a locally finite open refinement, using local extension of1 and support control.',
'proof fragment, continuing beyond supplied face','E','Cartan1951XV §§5–6 proves the classical fine/partition-of-unity framework, including the ring of arbitrary functions; Grothendieck1957 §3.3 supplies soft-sheaf extensions. The exact maximal-family continuation is absent, so the full general soft-ring argument is separately reconstructed in computations.md rather than claimed read on the sheet.','classical sheaf theory1950s','Established in the stated paracompact Hausdorff setting; not a theorem on arbitrary spaces or merely local softness without its globalization hypotheses.',['Cartan1951XV','Grothendieck1957'],'medium',comp='computations.md §21 gives a complete locally finite recursive construction.')
c(107,[84],[141,139],'1856–1862;1800–1802','F→F⊗I;f↦f⊗1;torsion-free lemma',
'If I is the sheaf of arbitrary integer-valued functions, F→F⊗_Z I is a monomorphism. At each stalk evaluation at the base point splits the map; the resulting tensor sheaf is fine in the standard paracompact setting.',
'proof sketched across reversed typed-page order','E','Cartan1951XV §5 Prop1 and§6 prove tensor preservation of fineness and fineness of the arbitrary-function ring; Cartan1951XVI p4 gives precisely the tensor embedding into a fine sheaf. The independent stalk retraction checks the actual arrow without demanding a global retraction.','1951','Established before the likely manuscript period. Exact typed provenance is not inferred merely from the theorem match.',['Cartan1951XV','Cartan1951XVI'],comp='computations.md §21 gives the stalk splitting and the alternative primitive-element torsion-free lemma.')
c(108,[84],[139],'1802–1806','extension à X de θ;G^Y',
'A fine sheaf on an open or closed subspace remains fine after the appropriate extension by zero to X; the local support endomorphisms extend by zero.',
'proof fragment in reused typescript','E','Cartan1951XV §§5–6 gives the local-support endomorphism definition; its pointwise extension proves the assertion with the chosen open j_! or closed i_* convention. This is distinct from a claim that all cohomology of the extension vanishes in the exotic relative curve site.','1951 fine-sheaf framework','Established for the standard topological sheaf setting and the stated extension functor.',['Cartan1951XV'],comp='computations.md §21 checks supports and the locally finite sum after extension.')

c(109,[84],[141,139],'1860–1862;1800','torsion-free H; a primitive; x↦x⊗a injective',
'If H is a torsion-free abelian group and a is nonzero and not divisible by any integer of absolute value>1 in H, then G→G⊗_Z H, x↦x⊗a, is injective for every abelian group G.',
'proof given across reversed typed-page order','E','The finitely generated case follows because a primitive vector extends to a Z-basis; passage through finitely generated subgroups gives the general case. The specific sheaf application is also in Cartan1951XVI p4. This is an algebraic proof, not an inference that every nonzero element of a torsion-free group is primitive.','classical abelian-group algebra;1951 sheaf application','Established with the primitive-element condition exactly retained.',['Cartan1951XVI'],comp='computations.md §21 gives both the finite-subgroup argument and the evaluation splitting in the actual application.')
c(110,[84],[141],'1854','if G fine then F⊗G fine',
'Tensoring a fine sheaf of abelian groups with any sheaf preserves fineness, since its support-controlled endomorphisms act on the second factor.',
'proof on reused typescript','E','Cartan1951XV §5 Prop1 states and proves this tensor property. The proof applies1⊗θ to each fine endomorphism; local finiteness and the identity sum are preserved.','1951','Established classical fine-sheaf construction.',['Cartan1951XV'],comp='computations.md §21 checks the support and identity conditions.')
c(111,[82],[137],'1754–1762','B/h(A) flat',
'For a flat ring map A→B, faithful flatness is equivalent to injectivity of A→B together with flatness of the A-module B/A.',
'proof fragment on reused typescript','E','Stacks00H9 gives faithful flatness and pure module-unit maps. The Tor exact sequence for0→A→B→B/A→0 proves the equivalence: Tor₁(B/A,M) is the kernel of M→B⊗M.','standard faithfully-flat algebra;SGA era','Established after recognizing the quotient; B h(A) is not a meaningful substitute in this exact sequence.',['Stacks00H9'],comp='computations.md §24 gives the Tor argument.')

